researchers say not enough minorities  are taking part in clinical trials for  coronavirus vaccines  even as minority groups are affected at  much higher rates  black americans are 4.7 times more  likely to be hospitalized with this  virus than white americans  then white americans and latinos are 4.6  times more likely  adriana diaz has been looking at  diversity and the treatment  the most important reason why i decided  to participate  is because how impacted my family  has been by the covet 19 pandemic  i lost my beloved father dr chris  purnell  watched kovitt kill her father and  infect her sister a breast cancer  survivor who is still  recovering that pushed her to enroll in  a vaccine clinical trial  i felt like this was my way to  partly honor my father's legacy what's  the reaction that you get  if there is that pause are you sure you  should do this is it safe  um you should wait like i don't think  that skepticism  um can be um lessened necessarily  um i say embrace it and then inform  people with the facts  in the u.s three vaccine candidates are  currently in phase three trials  meaning volunteers receive vaccine  candidates to see if they work  while latinos represent more than 18  percent of the u.s population  and african americans more than 13  percent in pfizer's u.s trial  only 11 percent of participants are  latino and 8 percent are  african-american  the numbers are slightly better in  modernis trial but not by much  astrazeneca has not publicly provided a  racial breakdown of its  trials dr anthony fauci says  representation isn't just about  optics it's about effectiveness we've  got to get  a higher percentage of minorities into  the clinical trial  so that when we get the data we can say  that it applies equally this pandemic  is black his brown  is white that's why the national  institutes of health is funding a media  campaign to recruit trial participants  especially minorities the agency may  have a lot of convincing to do  after centuries of systematic racism in  american health care  perhaps most notoriously the tuskegee  syphilis study  where from 1932 to 1972 the u.s  government withheld medical treatment  from  hundreds of black men who had syphilis  as part of an experiment  even decades after a treatment existed  to help repair trust  reverend edwin sanders is spearheading  an nih-funded initiative that works with  faith-based leaders  to disseminate accurate information  about cova vaccine development what  we're trying to do  is to make sure that people have a level  of knowledge that allows them to make  informed decisions  i think if we invest in engaging with  the communities  that we want to see enrolled in our  clinical trials then  trust won't be the barrier dr helle  borno  just released a study on diversity in  covid research and clinical trials  and found that in all cases african  americans are underrepresented  under recruitment is the primary problem  i think it's an unacceptable cop-out to  say that it's  just because of mistrust some may also  worry about the potential side effects  of an  unapproved treatment but for child  participant dr purnell  it's worth it many people just think  well i'm not going to take that risk  i'll let someone else  do it look i'm willing to be that  someone else we need to be able to get  at an answer and i want to help us get  there  dr borno told us that her research  indicates that when specific groups are  disproportionately affected by a disease  in her experience are also more likely  to participate  in related clinical research that is  good news for the kova trial happening  here at the university of illinois  chicago  which is specifically focused on  enrolling minorities  anyone who wants to learn more about  potentially participating can go to  preventcovid.org this story certainly  got me thinking about it gail  yeah i hear you adriana such a very  important story i'm so glad you're doing  it and bravo to dr pernell  really important work she's doing
 

